--TEST--
A `static` in a parameter type is the class the call names, at a static call and at an instance call, and inside the body a `property<static>` is the property's name as a `string` — `rule:statements/static-is-a-member-modifier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `static` in a parameter type is the class that the call names.
// `Category::sortBy` takes the names of `Category`'s properties.
class Model {
    public string $id = "m1";

    public static function sortBy(property<static> $field): string {
        // Inside the method, `$field` is the property name as a `string`.
        return "sorted by " . $field;
    }

    public function countLike(array<static> $others): int {
        return Core\Arr::count($others) as int;
    }
}

class Category extends Model {
    public int $rank = 3;
}

// `rank` is a property of `Category`, so this call compiles.
echo Category::sortBy("rank" as property<Category>), "\n";

// A property of `Model` is also a property of `Category`.
echo Category::sortBy("id" as property<Model>), "\n";

// At an instance call, `static` is the class of the object.
// `countLike` takes an `array<Category>` here.
Category $a = new Category();
array<Category> $others = [new Category(), new Category()];
echo $a->countLike($others) as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
sorted by rank
sorted by id
2
